  "content": {
    "id": "p32-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "$S=\\{A,B,C,D,E\\}$ and $U=\\{A,B,C,D\\}$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p32-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Write down all the 3-letter subsets of $S$ that do not contain $E$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\{A,B,C\\},\\ \\{A,B,D\\},\\ \\{A,C,D\\},\\ \\{B,C,D\\}$",
          "worked": "Choose three elements from $A,B,C,D$:\n$$\\{A,B,C\\},\\quad\\{A,B,D\\},\\quad\\{A,C,D\\},\\quad\\{B,C,D\\}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Explain why this is a list of all the 3-letter subsets of $U$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$U=S\\setminus\\{E\\}$, so the 3-letter subsets of $S$ without $E$ are exactly the 3-letter subsets of $U$.",
          "worked": "$U$ contains exactly the elements of $S$ other than $E$. Every listed subset therefore belongs to $U$, and every 3-letter subset of $U$ is a 3-letter subset of $S$ that does not contain $E$. Thus the lists are identical.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Write down all the 3-letter subsets of $S$ that contain $E$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\{A,B,E\\},\\ \\{A,C,E\\},\\ \\{A,D,E\\},\\ \\{B,C,E\\},\\ \\{B,D,E\\},\\ \\{C,D,E\\}$",
          "worked": "Include $E$ and choose two elements from $A,B,C,D$:\n$$\\begin{aligned}&\\{A,B,E\\},\\quad\\{A,C,E\\},\\quad\\{A,D,E\\},\\\\&\\{B,C,E\\},\\quad\\{B,D,E\\},\\quad\\{C,D,E\\}.\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Explain how to pair them up with all the 2-letter subsets of $U$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Remove $E$ from each 3-letter subset. Conversely, add $E$ to each 2-letter subset of $U$; this gives a one-to-one pairing.",
          "worked": "Each 3-letter subset containing $E$ has the form $\\{X,Y,E\\}$, where $\\{X,Y\\}$ is a 2-letter subset of $U$. Remove $E$ to obtain the pairings:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\{A,B,E\\}&\\leftrightarrow\\{A,B\\}\\\\\\{A,C,E\\}&\\leftrightarrow\\{A,C\\}\\\\\\{A,D,E\\}&\\leftrightarrow\\{A,D\\}\\\\\\{B,C,E\\}&\\leftrightarrow\\{B,C\\}\\\\\\{B,D,E\\}&\\leftrightarrow\\{B,D\\}\\\\\\{C,D,E\\}&\\leftrightarrow\\{C,D\\}.\\end{aligned}$$\nAdding $E$ reverses each pairing, so every 2-letter subset of $U$ occurs exactly once.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q6-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Hence explain why ${}^{4}C_{2}+{}^{4}C_{3}={}^{5}C_{3}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The 3-letter subsets of $S$ either contain $E$ (${ }^{4}C_{2}$ choices) or do not contain $E$ (${ }^{4}C_{3}$ choices). These disjoint cases give ${}^{4}C_{2}+{}^{4}C_{3}={}^{5}C_{3}$.",
          "worked": "${}^{5}C_{3}$ counts all 3-letter subsets of $S$. Split them into two disjoint categories:\n\nThose not containing $E$ are exactly the 3-letter subsets of $U$, counted by ${}^{4}C_{3}$.\n\nThose containing $E$ pair with the 2-letter subsets of $U$, counted by ${}^{4}C_{2}$.\n\nThese categories include every 3-letter subset of $S$ exactly once. Therefore,\n$${}^{4}C_{2}+{}^{4}C_{3}={}^{5}C_{3}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q6-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Explain why ${}^{n}C_{r-1}+{}^{n}C_{r}={}^{n+1}C_{r}$ for any whole numbers $n$ and $r$ with $1\\leq r\\leq n$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Distinguish one element $X$ in an $(n+1)$-element set. The $r$-member subsets containing $X$ number ${}^{n}C_{r-1}$; those without $X$ number ${}^{n}C_r$. Together they count all ${}^{n+1}C_r$ subsets.",
          "worked": "${}^{n+1}C_r$ counts the $r$-member subsets of an $(n+1)$-element set. Label one element $X$ and divide the subsets into two disjoint categories:\n\nSubsets not containing $X$ consist of $r$ of the remaining $n$ elements, giving ${}^{n}C_r$ choices.\n\nSubsets containing $X$ are obtained by choosing $r-1$ of the remaining $n$ elements and adding $X$, giving ${}^{n}C_{r-1}$ choices.\n\nEvery $r$-member subset belongs to exactly one category. Therefore,\n$${}^{n}C_{r-1}+{}^{n}C_r={}^{n+1}C_r.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      }
    ]
  },
